8,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,2,8,8,8,3,3],[3,3,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,3,3],[3,3,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,3,3],[3,3,8,8,8,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,8,8,8,3,3],[3,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,3],[3,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,3],[3,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grid is made of.** Every input row is filled with one colour, so the input is really just a list of colours read from top to bottom. Each output is a square made of concentric square rings.\n\n**How the output is built.** Ring *d* is the set of cells whose distance to the nearest border is *d*. In each training pair, ring *d* takes the colour of input row *d*.\n\n- **Pair 1:**\n  - Rows 0–1 are 8, and the outer two rings are 8.\n  - Rows 2–6 are 2, and rings 2–6 are 2.\n  - Row 7 is 6, and ring 7 is 6.\n  - Rows 8–9 are 8, and rings 8–9 are 8.\n  - Rows 10–11 are 1, and rings 10–11 are 1.\n  - Row 12 is 2, and it forms the 2×2 centre.\n- **Pair 2:** This works the same way, and the 8 in row 11 becomes the 2×2 centre.\n\n**Output size.**\n- Pair 1 has H=14 and the output is 26×26.\n- Pair 2 has H=13 and the output is 24×24.\n\nSo the side is N = 2·(H−1). The last input row repeats the colour of the row above it, and it is not used. The deepest ring, index H−2, is the 2×2 centre. Input width plays no role.\n\n**Test.** The test input has H=14, so the output is 26×26 with these rings from outside to centre:\n- rings 0–1: colour 3\n- rings 2–4: colour 8\n- ring 5: colour 2\n- rings 6–7: colour 8\n- ring 8: colour 3\n- rings 9–10: colour 7\n- ring 11: colour 5\n- ring 12 (the 2×2 centre): colour 8\n\n**Construction stages.** The program first allocates the canvas filled with row 0's colour. It then paints each run of same-coloured rings as one stage, working inward.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.asarray(grid)\n    H = g.shape[0]\n    cols = [int(g[r, 0]) for r in range(H)]\n    N = 2 * (H - 1)\n    yy, xx = np.indices((N, N))\n    depth = np.minimum(np.minimum(yy, xx), np.minimum(N - 1 - yy, N - 1 - xx))\n    out = np.full((N, N), cols[0], dtype=int)\n    emit(out, f\"Allocate a {N}x{N} square (2*(H-1)) filled with row 0's colour {cols[0]}: the outermost ring.\")\n    maxd = N // 2 - 1\n    d = 1\n    while d <= maxd:\n        e = d\n        while e + 1 <= maxd and cols[e + 1] == cols[d]:\n            e += 1\n        mask = (depth >= d) & (depth <= e)\n        out[mask] = cols[d]\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Rings {d}..{e} take the colour {cols[d]} of input rows {d}..{e}.\")\n        d = e + 1\n    emit(out, \"Final nested squares: ring d has the colour of input row d.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Output size.**"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Construction stages.** The program first allocates the canvas filled with row 0's colour."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each output is a square made of concentric square rings."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Rows 0–1 are 8, and the outer two rings are 8."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Rows 2–6 are 2, and rings 2–6 are 2."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"aa0780e34edef3a17eae66d14d088fb098b2b1ae65e67914e54dd49f90b768fc","task_sha256":"47a8b6debc22edc602630ace982d228526badd0b6bbcbb98f668db4f2ea028ce","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
